  "answer_text": "A **Custodial IRA** (often referred to as an \"IRA for Kids\") is an Individual Retirement Account that an adult opens and manages for a minor child (usually under age 18 or 21, depending on the state). \n\nBecause minors cannot legally open brokerage accounts or manage investments on their own, an adult acts as the **custodian**. The custodian handles the account, makes investment decisions, and manages the funds until the child reaches the age of majority, at which point control of the account transitions entirely to the child.\n\n---\n\n### Key Rules of a Custodial IRA\n\n#### 1. The \"Earned Income\" Requirement\nThis is the most critical rule of a Custodial IRA: **the child must have earned income to contribute.** \n* **What counts:** Income from a formal job (W-2 payroll) or informal work (like babysitting, mowing lawns, pet sitting, or modeling). \n* **What does NOT count:** Allowance, cash gifts, investment returns, or passive income. \n* **Funding the account:** While the child must have earned income, *anyone* can actually put the money into the account. For example, if a teenager earns $3,000 working a summer job, a parent or grandparent can contribute $3,000 to their Custodial IRA so the child can keep and spend their hard-earned cash. \n\n#### 2. Contribution Limits\nThe maximum amount you can contribute to a Custodial IRA in a given tax year is **the lesser of**:\n* The child's total earned income for that year.\n* The maximum annual IRS contribution limit.\n\n*For example, the standard annual IRA limits are:*\n* **2025 limit:** $7,000\n* **2026 limit:** $7,500\n\n*(Example: If a child earns $2,500 in 2026, the maximum contribution is $2,500. If they earn $10,000, the maximum contribution is $7,500.)*\n\n---\n\n### Types of Custodial IRAs: Traditional vs. Roth\n\nLike standard IRAs, you can choose between two main types, though one is far more popular for children:\n\n* **Custodial Roth IRA (Most Recommended):** Contributions are made with after-tax money. Because children usually earn very little and are in the lowest tax bracket (often paying $0 in income taxes), they don\u2019t benefit from a tax deduction. Instead, they get the ultimate benefit: the money grows tax-free, and they can withdraw it completely **tax-free** in retirement.\n* **Custodial Traditional IRA:** Contributions are made with pre-tax dollars (potentially lowering current taxable income), but withdrawals in retirement are taxed as ordinary income. This is rarely ideal for minors since their current tax rates are already extremely low.\n\n---\n\n### Benefits of a Custodial IRA\n\n* **The Power of Compounding:** Because the child is starting decades before the average adult, their money has significantly more time to compound. A few thousand dollars invested in a child's teens can easily grow into hundreds of thousands (or even millions) of dollars by the time they retire, even if they never add another cent.\n* **Roth IRA Withdrawal Flexibility:** Roth IRAs offer unique flexibility. You can always withdraw the *contributions* (the original money put in) tax- and penalty-free at any time. Furthermore, up to $10,000 of the *earnings* can be withdrawn penalty-free to buy a first home, or used for qualified higher education expenses.\n* **Financial Literacy:** It provides an excellent, real-world tool to teach children about saving, investing, stock markets, and the power of compound interest.\n\n---\n\n### What happens when the child turns 18 or 21?\nOnce the child reaches the legal age of majority in their state (usually 18 or 21), the custodial status ends. The account is transferred into their name as a standard IRA, and they assume full control over how the money is invested or spent.",
